From 8672b553e31fb4da2ca1a5749fb98cf00364dde9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yvan Janssens <yvanj@cyber.gent>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 12:40:14 +0100
Subject: [RFC PATCH 6/7] KVM: s390: Add KVM_CAP_S390_ESA390_CM

Operating systems and stand-alone programs that were written before the
configuration-z/Architecture-architectural-mode facility expect to be
loaded in ESA/390 mode: they take their first interruptions through the
eight-byte PSWs at real 0-127 and issue SIGP set-architecture to get to
z/Architecture. A KVM guest is always in z/Architecture mode, so such a
program takes its first interruption through a new PSW it never set up,
and its set-architecture order is rejected. The architecture has a mode
for exactly this, the ESA/390-compatibility mode, which a machine provides
to the guests of a hypervisor: a state description without the
z/Architecture flag is run in it.

Add the VM capability KVM_CAP_S390_ESA390_CM and the device attribute
group KVM_S390_VM_ARCH_MODE, with which user space puts all vCPUs of the
configuration into the mode and back.

KVM does as little as it can. Setting the mode changes the format of the
state descriptions (z/Architecture flag, enhanced DAT, and the execution
controls of facilities that exist only in z/Architecture), aligns the
prefix, and changes facility bit 2, which tells the program whether the
z/Architecture architectural mode is active: KVM keeps that bit in line
with the mode wherever it presents the facility list (to STORE FACILITY
LIST, to STORE FACILITY LIST EXTENDED through the state description, and
in KVM_S390_VM_CPU_PROCESSOR). All other facility bits, among them 138
and 168, which describe whether the configuration offers the mode at all,
are set by user space in its CPU model as before. The eight-byte PSW is
put into the state description around SIE; everything else in KVM and the
whole user space interface keep the 16-byte PSW. What the architecture
defines for a change of the mode beyond that is left to user space, which
has to handle SIGP itself for such a VM: the checks and register changes
of the set-architecture order, the captured z/Architecture state, the
ESA/390 formats of the store-status orders. A change of the mode needs all
vCPUs outside of KVM_RUN and fails with -EBUSY otherwise, so no vCPU sees
it in the middle of an interception.

The capability is offered on every host, like KVM_CAP_S390_VSIE_ESAMODE.
Both depend on the same property: the SIE instruction of this host runs a
state description without the z/Architecture flag. With vsie it is the
shadow state description of a nested guest, here the state description of
a guest of KVM itself; the machine (or the hypervisor below) sees the same
format either way. KVM_CAP_S390_VSIE_ESAMODE is offered unconditionally,
since no facility bit, SCLP indication or machine generation describes
that property: facility bit 168 describes a guest configuration, and bit
138 is set by KVM for its own guests on every machine. A host that does
not run such a state description answers it with a validity interception,
and KVM_RUN fails with -EINVAL (with a message instead of a warning, see
the previous patch); the host is not affected. Requiring a module
parameter as well would only make the administrator state what KVM cannot
check either.

Not supported in the mode: guest debugging (refused when the mode is set
while it is enabled for a vCPU), protected and user-controlled VMs.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5-2
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
