diff --git a/Documentation/virt/kvm/api.rst b/Documentation/virt/kvm/api.rst
index e0430cc750c9..2f7310c12689 100644
--- a/Documentation/virt/kvm/api.rst
+++ b/Documentation/virt/kvm/api.rst
@@ -9056,6 +9056,69 @@ enabled, cmma can't be enabled anymore and pfmfi and the storage key
 interpretation are disabled. If cmma has already been enabled or the
 hpage_2g module parameter is not set to 1, -EINVAL is returned.
 
+7.48 KVM_CAP_S390_ESA390_CM
+---------------------------
+
+:Architectures: s390
+:Type: vm
+:Parameters: none
+:Returns: 0 on success; -EINVAL if KVM_CAP_S390_USER_SIGP is not enabled or
+          the VM has the KVM_VM_S390_UCONTROL flag set; -EBUSY if vCPUs were
+          already created for the VM.
+
+This capability lets the configuration be put into the ESA/390-compatibility
+mode, the architectural mode in which a machine that otherwise only provides
+z/Architecture runs programs written for ESA/390 that do not use dynamic
+address translation. Operating systems and stand-alone programs that are
+loaded in ESA/390 mode and switch to z/Architecture themselves with SIGNAL
+PROCESSOR (set architecture) need it.
+
+In the mode, the vCPUs run on state descriptions without the z/Architecture
+flag. As for KVM_CAP_S390_VSIE_ESAMODE, KVM cannot tell in advance whether
+the machine, or the hypervisor KVM runs under, accepts such state
+descriptions. If it does not, KVM_RUN fails with -EINVAL after a validity
+interception.
+
+The VM stays in z/Architecture mode until user space sets the mode through
+the device attribute group KVM_S390_VM_ARCH_MODE (see
+Documentation/virt/kvm/devices/vm.rst).
+
+Facility bit 2 (z/Architecture architectural mode active) is maintained by
+KVM once the capability is enabled: it is zero while the configuration is in
+the mode and one otherwise, in the facility list the guest sees (STORE
+FACILITY LIST, STORE FACILITY LIST EXTENDED) and in the one returned by
+KVM_S390_VM_CPU_PROCESSOR. Every other facility bit is the responsibility of
+user space, which sets it through KVM_S390_VM_CPU_PROCESSOR as usual. A
+configuration that is to offer the mode architecturally has bit 168
+(ESA/390-compatibility-mode facility) one and bit 138
+(configuration-z/Architecture-architectural-mode facility) zero; KVM sets
+bit 138 when the VM is created and does not change either bit for this
+capability.
+
+While the configuration is in the mode
+
+- KVM presents interruptions with eight-byte old and new PSWs and the other
+  ESA/390 assigned storage locations, uses a prefix area of 4K bytes and
+  applies low-address protection to locations 0-511 only;
+- all interfaces keep exchanging the PSW in the 16-byte z/Architecture format
+  (struct kvm_run, KVM_S390_SET_INITIAL_PSW). Bit 12 is zero in that format,
+  bits 97-127 hold the instruction address. A PSW with the DAT bit or the
+  extended-addressing-mode bit is invalid in the mode;
+- the prefix (KVM_SYNC_PREFIX, KVM_REG_S390_PREFIX) is a multiple of 4K;
+- the high halves of the general and control registers are not used. User
+  space is responsible for the register contents that the architecture
+  defines for a change of the mode;
+- SIGP orders that KVM executes itself and that exist only in z/Architecture
+  are rejected as invalid orders. All other orders, including set
+  architecture, are handled by user space, which has to use the ESA/390
+  formats (for instance for store status);
+- KVM_S390_STORE_STATUS returns -EINVAL, the guest cannot start interpretive
+  execution, and the VM cannot become a protected VM (KVM_PV_ENABLE returns
+  -EINVAL once the capability is enabled);
+- guest debugging is not supported: KVM_SET_GUEST_DEBUG with
+  KVM_GUESTDBG_ENABLE returns -EINVAL, and setting the mode returns -EINVAL
+  while guest debugging is enabled for any vCPU.
+
 8. Other capabilities.
 ======================
 
